{"question": "A 41-year-old G3P1 woman presents with a sudden onset throbbing headache, tinnitus, nausea, and left-sided weakness. Patient has no significant past medical history and takes no medications.  Her last two pregnancies ended with spontaneous abortions before the 10th week of gestation. No significant family history. Her vital signs include: blood pressure 130/90 mm Hg, pulse 58/min, respiratory rate 11/min, and temperature 36.8\u2103 (98.2\u2109). GCS is 14/15. Physical examination shows 3+ deep tendon reflexes and increased muscle tone in the left upper and lower extremities. Laboratory findings are significant for the following:\nPlatelet count 230,000/mm3\nFibrinogen 3.5 g/L\nActivated partial thromboplastin time  70 s\nThrombin time 34 s\n A non-contrast CT of the head is performed and shown in the picture. Which of the following would be the next best diagnostic step in this patient?", "options": {"A": "Mixing study", "B": "INR", "C": "D-dimer", "D": "Ristocetin-induced platelet aggregation test", "E": "Clot retraction study"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Neurology", "Obstetrics and Gynecology", "Hematology", "Emergency Medicine", "Radiology"], "option_domains": ["Neurology", "Hematology"], "question_analyses": {"Neurology": "Based on the clinical presentation and the laboratory findings provided, the patient is likely experiencing a subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH), which is a life-threatening condition characterized by a sudden onset of a severe headache, often described as \"the worst headache of my life,\" along with other symptoms such as nausea, vomiting, and neurological deficits. The physical examination findings, including left-sided weakness and increased muscle tone with hyperreflexia, are consistent with a neurological deficit that could be due to a SAH affecting the brainstem or the left cerebral hemisphere. The laboratory findings, particularly the prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) an