{"question": "A medical researcher is studying the physiology of the immune system in order to better understand the effects of HIV on patients. He isolates a group of cells that are shown by flow cytometry to be positive for the cell surface marker CD8. He then mixes this cell population with a group of infected cells, crosslinks extracellular interactions, and immunoprecipitates the CD8 protein. He identifies a protein bound to CD8 that is composed of two chains from an adjacent cell. Which of the following best describes the primary function of the protein that was most likely identified?", "options": {"A": "Binds complement proteins on the cell surface", "B": "Binds endogenous peptides that are present in the endosome", "C": "Binds endogenous peptides that have been transported by the TAP channel", "D": "Binds exogenous peptides that are present in the endosome", "E": "Binds exogenous peptides that have been transported by the TAP channel"}, "pred_answer": "C", "gold_answer": "C", "question_domains": ["Immunology", "Virology", "Hematology", "Molecular Biology", "Pathology"], "option_domains": ["Immunology", "Molecular Biology"], "question_analyses": {"Immunology": "In the medical scenario presented, the researcher is studying the effects of HIV on the immune system, focusing on CD8-positive T cells, which are a key component of the adaptive immune response. The researcher's methodology involves isolating CD8-positive cells, mixing them with infected cells, crosslinking extracellular interactions, and then immunoprecipitating the CD8 protein to identify associated proteins. The identification of a protein composed of two chains from an adjacent cell suggests that the protein identified is likely to be part of the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) class I molecules, specifically the \u03b22-microglobulin (\u03b22m) chain. ### Interpretation of the Condition\n\n1. **CD8-Positive T Cells**: These cells are crucial for the immune response, particularly in the co